why 2 7/16 SPACING The tenor runs down C–B♭–A–G in sixteenths while the alto holds A; the gap opens for one sixteenth before the cadence.
why 3 3/8 CROSSING The tenor passes down B♭–A♭–G under the bass's held B♭ for one eighth. On the next beat the bass drops to E♭ and the order is back.
why 3 1/2 DOUBLING Alto (F–G) and tenor (A♭–G) meet on G by step; the tenor's A♭ was a passing note that has to fall.
why 5 0 PARALLEL_OCTAVES Soprano and alto go from F to G an octave apart, but the alto fills the beat with a figure, F–B♭–A♭–G. Bach treats the decoration as enough to separate the lines; strict exercises still count it.
why 7 1/8 SPACING The alto climbs to C as a neighbor note while the tenor sits low on A and G. The gap lasts a beat; then the tenor climbs C–D.
why 7 1/4 SPACING Still the alto high on B♭ over the low tenor G, in a bright i chord.
why 7 3/8 SPACING The alto steps down to A and the tenor rises to C on the next beat, closing the gap.
why 8 0 PARALLEL_FIFTHS Soprano and tenor fall B♭–A and E♭–D a fifth apart, the soprano an eighth earlier. Staggered like this Bach accepts them; strict exercises still count them.
why 11 1/8 CROSSING Alto and tenor trade places: the tenor leaps up to G as the alto falls G–E♭–C. Each line keeps its own shape over the bass's descent.
why 11 1/8 SPACING The bass walks down C–B♭–A♭–G toward the fermata while the tenor has leapt up high, so the gap between them is wide for a beat.
why 11 1/4 CROSSING The alto drops further, to C, while the tenor holds G above it.
why 11 1/4 SPACING The bass's descent reaches A♭ while the tenor is still high on G.
why 11 3/8 CROSSING The tenor steps down G–F as the alto waits on C; at the fermata the alto rises to G and is on top again.
why 11 3/8 SPACING The tenor steps down to F; at the fermata it drops to B and the gap closes.
why 13 0 RANGE The bass climbs B♭–C–D and then drops an octave to D for V. The high D carries the ⁶₄ chord; the drop gives the cadence weight.
